@page
@{
	ViewData["Title"] = "Dakar 2000";
	Layout = "~/Pages/Shared/_Layout.cshtml";
}

	<div class="text-center">
		<h1>
			Summary (2000 - Paris - Dakar - Cairo)
		</h1>
		<p>
			<img src="2000_map.jpg" alt="" style="float:right;" />
			<img src="2000_01.jpg" alt="" style="float:left;" />
			The Dakar 2000 was intended to be one of the most challenging rallies ever, and so
			a completely new route was planned, leading from Dakar in Senegal right across the
			African continent and ending in Cairo. In between, 7,626
			km of special stages and 10,905 km of total distance, with passages over 800 km long,
			had to be overcome. After the cars had been shipped from Paris to Dakar, it seemed
			as if the Pajeros would once again put their stamp on the race. Despite the
			high speeds the route was demanding, and so technique and
			driving skill were in greater demand than the car. The Mitsubishis took turns
			at the front, separated only by minutes. For Fontenay, misfortune took
			its course when he took a wrong turn near Burkina Faso and got completely lost.
			<img src="2000_02.jpg" alt="" style="float:right;" />
			<img src="2000_03.jpg" alt="" style="float:left;" />
			On top of that, his Pajero was plagued by brake problems. After the sixth
			stage, Shinozuka led by 6m 15s over second-placed Jean-Louis Schlesser. From then on the rally
			changed its face, for now the stages began in the Sahel pre-desert,
			where the dusty bush ends and the first sand dunes along the Niger
			appear. The Mitsubishis fought doggedly, but then the first
			of two disasters occurred. Heavy terrorist activity was reported from Niger,
			and the organisation, concerned for the safety of the rally community, cancelled
			the next five days of the rally. That was the first time in the 22-year history
			of the Dakar that such a thing had happened. Three Russian Antonov 127 aircraft were brought in
			and the 336 vehicles and 1,365 participants were flown within five days to Sabah
			in Libya, where the rally was continued on 17 January with the real desert stages.
			The second misfortune occurred when, on the 12th stage, enormous downpours
			<img src="2000_04.jpg" alt="" style="float:right;" />
			<img src="2000_05.jpg" alt="" style="float:left;" />
			and cold turned the sandy route into solid motorways. The Dakar transformed
			from a rally into a high-speed race, and so two or three hundred
			kilometres were covered at top speed. A catastrophe for the Mitsubishis,
			for the buggies, more than 600 kg lighter, were now vastly superior and
			pulled away. The high speed soon demanded a price, however, and
			four vehicles rolled over while jumping the crest of a dune. Among the crashed
			cars were three Mitsubishis, piloted by Shinozuka, Sousa and Prieto.
			The fourth car was a Nissan with Gregoire de Mevius at the
			wheel. A helicopter of the medical team took the drivers and navigators to
			hospital and the rally continued without the four. Team Mitsubishi France
			celebrated a very good third place at the Dakar 2000 on arrival in Cairo,
			after pushing their Pajeros to the limit, for the Dakar 2000 was one
			of the fastest there had ever been, which above all benefited the light Schlesser
			buggy, which then also rolled through the finish first. After the rally,
			the drivers agreed that the rally was unfortunately one of the fastest there
			had ever been. Driving skill was neglected after the first half.
			Nevertheless, the Dakar 2000 was a spectacular event with as many highs
			and lows as a thriller.
		</p>
